Solve the equations:
(1) $3x - 5(x - 2) = 2$;
(2) $\frac{2x + 1}{3} - \frac{x - 2}{4} = 1$.

Given two quadratic equations in terms of $x$:
Equation ①: $x^{2} + (2k - 1)x + k^{2} - 2k + \frac{13}{2} = 0$
Equation ②: $x^{2} - (k + 2)x + 2k + \frac{9}{4} = 0$
(1) If both equations ① and ② have real roots, find the minimum integer value of $k$;
(2) If only one of equations ① and ② has real roots, state which equation has no real roots and explain the reason;
(3) Under the condition of (2), if $k$ is a positive integer, find the roots of the equation that has real roots.
